    \kicker{Contents}

    \meta[style=bignum]{01}
    \meta[style=entry]{The noisy room}
    \meta[style=toc-line]{Why the mess in your head is the raw material of every good thought. *Jonah Feld*}

    \meta[style=bignum]{02}
    \meta[style=entry]{Where ideas live}
    \meta[style=toc-line]{Desks, workshops, libraries and long walks: a photo essay. *Picture editor: Sami Hale*}

    \meta[style=bignum]{03}
    \meta[style=entry]{The slow spark}
    \meta[style=toc-line]{A lamp maker's drawers, and the myth of the sudden idea. *Tamsin Roe*}

    \meta[style=bignum]{04}
    \meta[style=entry]{The underliner}
    \meta[style=toc-line]{An archivist on pencils, second readings and paying attention. *Interview by Mira Okafor*}

    \meta[style=bignum]{05}
    \meta[style=entry]{Tools of the trade}
    \meta[style=toc-line]{Five humble things that help ideas along. *The editors*}

    \meta[style=bignum]{06}
    \meta[style=entry]{Ink}
    \meta[style=toc-line]{A last page on putting it down on paper. *Mira Okafor*}
  }
  \frame[cols=7-12, cols@tablet=1-8, cols@phone=all, top=2bl, top@tablet=auto, top@phone=auto, measure=620px]{
    \kicker{From the editor}
    \headline[style=headline-mid]{We are all collectors}

    Every issue of a magazine begins with a pile. Ours began with a shoebox on my desk: cuttings, receipts with words on the back, a postcard of a lamp, a photograph of a library I have never visited. None of it was a plan. All of it, it turned out, was this issue.

    We set out to make an issue about ideas without pretending to know where they come from. Nobody does, not really. But we found that the people who have them seem to share a few habits: they collect without knowing why, they come back to things, and they go for a lot of walks.

    So that is what you will find here. Jonah Feld on the noisy room inside your head. A lamp maker who has kept every drawing for nineteen years. An archivist who has worn down a few thousand pencils. A small, practical list of tools. And a last page, about ink.

    Read it slowly, if you can. Underline things. Come back to it in a month and see what you notice the second time.

    \byline{Mira Okafor, Editor}
  }
}

