\scene[name=places-b, palette=night]{
  \group[layout=row, cols=all, valign=text, gap=24px]{
    \figure[src=./pictures/woodworker.jpg, alt="A woodworker leaning over a bench, shaping a piece of wood with a hand tool", min-width=6col, ratio=4:3]{
      \caption{Hands busy, mind free: the cold-morning jobs.}
      \credit{Bailey Alexander / Unsplash}
    }
    \frame[min-width=4col]{
      \subhead{The second desk}

      Many of the people we asked keep two places to work: one for making and one for thinking. The making place is noisy, practical and often shared. The thinking place is small, a little out of the way, and nobody else is allowed to tidy it.

      The two need each other. Ideas found at the quiet desk get tested at the noisy one, and the noisy one sends back problems that only the quiet desk can solve.
    }
  }
  \group[layout=row, cols=all, valign=stretch, gap=24px]{
    \figure[src=./pictures/rainy-window.jpg, alt="The corner of a laptop on a wooden sill, in front of a window streaked with rain, a town blurred beyond", min-width=6col, ratio=3:2]{
      \caption{Rain on the window: a good day for staying in and finishing something.}
      \credit{arcticworks / Unsplash}
    }
    \figure[src=./pictures/notebook-desk.jpg, alt="A person wearing headphones writing in a notebook at a desk", min-width=6col, ratio=3:2, focus="50% 45%"]{
      \caption{Headphones on: a room of one's own, carried anywhere.}
      \credit{Marina Marcato / Unsplash}
    }
  }
}

