%% ======================================================================================
%% 8. Tools of the trade
%% ======================================================================================

\scene[name=tools, palette=sage]{
  \figure[src=./illustrations/keyboard.png, alt="A collage keyboard whose keys are scraps of paper in pink, blue, orange and printed text", cols=7-12, cols@tablet=3-8, cols@phone=all, top=0, max-width=640px]
  \frame[cols=1-6, cols@tablet=1-8, cols@phone=all, top=2bl]{
    \kicker{Roundup · 05}
    \headline[style=headline-mid]{Tools of the trade}
    \deck{Five humble things that help ideas along. None of them is new, and none of them needs charging.}
  }
  \group[layout=row, cols=all, valign=top, gap=24px]{
    \group[layout=stack, min-width=4col, gap=12px]{
      \figure[src=./pictures/pencils.jpg, alt="Coloured pencils standing in a white ceramic jar", ratio=4:3]{\credit{Debby Hudson / Unsplash}}
      \frame{
        \subhead[style=island]{\span[style=island-num]{01} A jar of pencils}
        Pencils forgive. You can write in the margin of anything, you can rub it out, and you never have to wonder whether one is going to run dry in the middle of a thought. Keep a jar on every surface you work at.
      }
    }
    \group[layout=stack, min-width=4col, gap=12px]{
      \figure[src=./pictures/pen-notebook.jpg, alt="A black and gold pen resting on an open notebook", ratio=4:3, focus="50% 50%"]{\credit{Tony Litvyak / Unsplash}}
      \frame{
        \subhead[style=island]{\span[style=island-num]{02} The cheap notebook}
        A notebook that is too beautiful stays empty. Buy the plain one, buy several, and give yourself permission to fill them with nonsense. The good lines are hiding among the bad ones.
      }
    }
    \group[layout=stack, min-width=4col, gap=12px]{
      \figure[src=./pictures/typewriter.jpg, alt="A beige and white typewriter on a table", ratio=4:3]{\credit{Luke Lung / Unsplash}}
      \frame{
        \subhead[style=island]{\span[style=island-num]{03} A machine with no internet}
        Anything that writes and does nothing else: an old typewriter, a laptop with the network switched off, a phone left in another room. The fewer doors there are, the fewer you will wander through.
      }
    }
    \group[layout=stack, min-width=4col, gap=12px]{
      \figure[src=./pictures/letter.jpg, alt="An old handwritten letter on a wooden table", ratio=4:3]{\credit{Simon Ray / Unsplash}}
      \frame{
        \subhead[style=island]{\span[style=island-num]{04} Letters to yourself}
        At the end of a working day, write a short note to the person who will sit down tomorrow. Where you stopped, what you were about to try, what is bothering you. It is the cheapest way to leave a door open.
      }
    }
    \group[layout=stack, min-width=4col, gap=12px]{
      \figure[src=./pictures/walk-trees.jpg, alt="A man walking along a road lined with bare trees", ratio=4:3, focus="50% 60%"]{\credit{Greysen Johnson / Unsplash}}
      \frame{
        \subhead[style=island]{\span[style=island-num]{05} A pair of shoes}
        The last tool is the one you leave the house in. When a problem will not move, move yourself. Twenty minutes is usually enough to turn the volume of the noisy room down without switching it off.
      }
    }
  }
}

%% ======================================================================================
%% 9. Ink: the closing page
%% ======================================================================================

\scene[name=ink, palette=night]{
  \figure[src=./illustrations/pen.png, alt="A collage fountain pen in greens and blues with handwriting on its barrel, its nib leaving a splash of blue ink", cols=1-6, cols@tablet=2-7, cols@phone=all, top=2bl, max-width=620px]
  \frame[cols=7-11, cols@tablet=2-7, cols@phone=all, top=4bl, top@tablet=auto, top@phone=auto, measure=620px]{
    \kicker{Last page · 06}
    \headline[style=headline-mid]{Ink}

    Sooner or later every idea has to leave the noisy room and go somewhere it can be seen. For most of history that place was paper, and the thing that carried it there was ink.

    I still write first drafts by hand. Not because it is better, but because it is slower, and the slowness lets me hear the sentence before I commit to it. A pen makes you choose. A keyboard lets you postpone choosing forever.

    The blot at the end of the line is the moment of commitment: the thought was in your head, and now it is on the page, and it is no longer only yours. That is a little frightening and entirely the point. Ideas that stay in the room never meet anyone. Put them down. Let them be wrong in public. Come back to them later and see what they have become. \endmark

    \byline{Mira Okafor}
  }
}

